      "dom_id": "question-80488",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The area of a square increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to its **perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to its *perimeter.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{(\\frac{k}{4s})}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{8{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}.\n$$",
